+# DOJ custody schedule for records supplied to the 9/11 Commission
+
+Public derivative of the November 9, 2026 Eastern source review · B129 / PD01 and PD04–PD05. An approved DOJ Executive Secretariat schedule identifies a permanent, segregated set of materials loaned, shown or briefed to the Commission. The successor item also covers how senior DOJ officials collected and prepared material for investigative bodies. These authorities give distinct transfer triggers and supply exact identifiers for seeking the DOJ inventory, transfer and receipt. [Original schedule](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/departments/department-of-justice/rg-0060/n1-060-05-001_sf115.pdf) · [Successor item 0002](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/departments/department-of-justice/rg-0060/daa-0060-2017-0034_sf115.pdf#page=4)
+
+| Source | Wording and recorded fields |
+| --- | --- |
+| **N1-060-05-001 item 1**, physical p. 2, extracted lines 45–58 | The permanent source set goes to the Washington National Records Center. The National Archives transfer trigger reads **“15 years after the end of the current Attorney General’s tenure.”** |
+| **NARA status sheet**, physical p. 1, lines 1–8 | Reported **11/01/2020**. It labels the schedule inactive and identifies **DAA-0060-2017-0034-0002** as the successor. The public RG-0060 index also labels the old schedule inactive. [RG-0060 index](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/index.html?dir=/departments/department-of-justice/rg-0060) |
+| **DAA-0060-2017-0034 item 0002**, physical p. 4, lines 9–57 | The active permanent item includes collected materials and senior leadership’s collection and preparation records, **“including search or compilation strategies.”** The supersession field cites **N1-060-05-001 / 1**. The accessioning trigger reads **“25 year(s) after completion of case or project.”** The initial transfer date-span field reads **“From 2002 To 2004.”** |
+| **Successor approval and pagination**, physical pp. 1 and 5 | The approval table gives **12/19/2018** and the creation footer **12/20/2018**. The retrieved PDF contains **five physical pages**. Its last visible footer reads **“Page 5 of 6.”** The sixth numbered page and the publication history remain unverified. |
+
+The triggers belong to separate authorities. The initial span describes the record dates expected in the initial transfer. The actual completion date used for the collection, applicable schedule assignment, transfer receipt and item-level contents remain open.
+
+## Separate routes and scope
+
+- The known **FAA N1-237-05-004** schedule distinguishes permanent originals and audio re-recordings from temporary scan and working-copy classes, physical pp. 2–3. The **Commission N1-148-04-001** schedule separately directs transfer of its own commissioners’, front-office and staff records at termination, physical pp. 5–6. Both PDF bodies match the earlier source pins byte for byte. [Earlier FAA review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/8bc80894a40ac207b2ccce62a9fdd6d22d4c1139/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-911-special-schedule-2026-10-30/README.md) · [Commission schedule](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/judicial-and-legislative/rg-0148/n1-148-04-001_sf115.pdf#page=5)
+- OSD **N1-330-04-002** item 1 excludes material subject to Executive privilege and uses transfer at **30 years old**, physical p. 1, lines 60–71. DHS **N1-563-05-001** item 1 uses transfer when the Commission’s records are transferred, physical p. 1, lines 49–69. Each scope and trigger is specific to its schedule. [OSD schedule](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/departments/department-of-defense/office-of-the-secretary-of-defense/rg-0330/n1-330-04-002_sf115.pdf) · [DHS schedule](https://www.archives.gov/files/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/departments/department-of-homeland-security/rg-0563/n1-563-05-001_sf115.pdf)
+- Five public RCS indexes were screened for September 11, terrorist attacks, 9/11 and the DOJ successor number. The FAA, DOJ, OSD and DHS authority entries were found. The OST **RG-0398** index yielded zero title hits, with **N1-398-00-001** found as a positive control. The screen covers captured table text, with unrelated hits retained in the ledger. It leaves separately titled and unindexed authorities and actual holdings open. [OST index](https://www.archives.gov/records-mgmt/rcs/schedules/index.html?dir=/departments/department-of-transportation/rg-0398) · [Source pins and index lines](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Open records
+
+- [ ] Locate the DOJ Executive Secretariat inventory, Washington National Records Center receipt, authority assignment and any later accession, using both schedule numbers. A dated item or enclosure match would identify an FAA-related counterpart and its transmission history. Seek a complete edition of the successor to check the pagination gap.
+- [ ] Carry forward the actual corrected May 2003 FAA answer and delivery acknowledgment, the signed consideration/action/no-action decision, and the recommendation-by-recommendation response and closure. The DOJ initial span ends before the August 2006 OIG recommendation. This review acquired no case-specific link from that set to a later correction. [OIG recommendations, physical pp. 12–13](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12) · [Case-specific queue](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/055ef54a857b46846d0038109bab7e108970f03a/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-postreport-disposition-2026-10-27/OPEN-RECORDS.md)
+
+Verified eleven response hashes, six PDF page counts and 21 text anchors. Inspected the cited passages, the DOJ predecessor’s transfer paragraph, the successor’s item 0002 and final page footer. The originals, exact access dates and unsuccessful first DNS attempts are preserved separately.
